    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
            <p xml:id="Aink432-kilavi">pirivu uṭampaṭṭum āṟṟāyākiṉṟatu eṉṉai eṉṟu viṉaviyavaḻi avar pōṉa curam pōkaṟku aritu eṉṟu āṟṟēṉ ākiṉṟēṉ eṉṟa talaimakaṭku vēṉiṟkālam kaḻintatu; kārpparuvattōṟṟattilē pirintār ākalāṉ accuram naṉṟu eṉat tōḻi colli āṟṟuvittatu.</p>
            <p xml:lang="en">After asking, 'And having agreed to the separation, why is it that you have become unable to endure [it]?', to the heroine who said, 'Thinking of the wasteland through which he goes that is difficult to traverse, I have become unable to endure [it]', so as to say, 'The hot season has passed; because he left when the rainy season appeared, that wasteland is good', the confidante reassured [the heroine] by speaking [this].<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">This <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kiḷavi</foreign> is appended to the previous poem, 431, but there is a note that it belongs to the whole decad. For the reader's convenience, it is provided here, although it is not in fact present in the edition.</note></p>
              </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink432">        
          <l>naṉṟē kātalar ceṉṟa vāṟē</l>
            <l>cuṭupo ṉaṉṉa koṉṟai cūṭik</l>
            <l>kaṭipuku vaṉarpōṉ maḷḷaru muṭaittē.</l>
        </lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
    <l>The way along which the lover went is good --</l>
    <l>it has warriors too like those entring a wedding,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">Seemingly <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kaṭi</foreign> is unattested in the Caṅkam corpus, or at least in those poems currently incorporated into the Tamilex database (as of 06.08.2026) in the meaning of wedding. Contextually though, it makes sense here that it refers to either a wedding or some other type of festivity, as that is when men wear garlands of laburnum flowers. The TL cites Maṇi 18.98 to support the definition of 'wedding'. It is glossed in Cōmasuntaraṉār's commentary with <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">viḻā</foreign>, which means 'festival'.</note></l>
    <l>wearing laburnum like glowing<note xml:lang="en" place="foot"><foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">cuṭu-tal</foreign> here may either refer to the process of heating up gold in order to refine it, in which case I would understand the comparison to be between laburnum flowers and refined gold; or it could refer simply to heated gold (firing is also a way to refine gold), in which case the comparison might be between the flowers and the specific colour gold assumes when heated.</note> gold.</l>
</lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
